{#
    Document the internal helpers, not only the public API.

    pdoc hides every name that starts with an underscore, and offers no flag to
    turn that off. The supported way to change it is to override `is_public`,
    which pdoc's default template defines as a macro for this purpose:
    https://pdoc.dev/docs/pdoc.html#how-can-i-include-private-members

    These pages are developer documentation for a codebase that has no outside
    users yet, so the reader is someone who has to change `_footer_manifest`,
    not someone choosing what to import. Hiding half the module from that reader
    is the wrong default. The Rust crate is rendered the same way: `cargo docs`
    passes `--document-private-items`, without which a binary crate documents
    almost nothing.

    The rules below are the default ones, with the last two swapped: a single
    leading underscore no longer hides a member, and dunders other than
    `__init__` still do, because those are Python's own machinery rather than
    anything written here.
#}
{% extends "default/module.html.jinja2" %}

{% defaultmacro is_public(doc) %}
    {% if "@private" in doc.docstring %}
        {# a member can still opt out by saying so in its docstring #}
    {% elif "@public" in doc.docstring %}
        true
    {% elif not include_undocumented and not doc.docstring and doc.kind != "module" %}
        {# --no-include-undocumented was passed #}
    {% elif doc.name == "__init__" and (doc.docstring or (doc.kind == "function" and doc.signature_without_self.parameters)) %}
        true
    {% elif doc.name.startswith("__") and doc.name.endswith("__") %}
        {# Python's own machinery, including __doc__ #}
    {% elif doc.kind == "module" and doc.fullname not in all_modules %}
        {# a module left off the command line #}
    {% elif doc.kind == "variable" and doc.is_typevar and not doc.docstring %}
        {# a bare TypeVar documents nothing #}
    {% else %}
        true
    {% endif %}
{% enddefaultmacro %}
